-- ---------------------------------------------------------------- through the compiler
-- #jit compiles a definition whose parameters cross its boundary (words, byte strings); AMem
-- does not cross (an allocation is a dependent pair: its bytes' length is its size's), so the
-- compiled definition takes the array's bytes, n and the fuel, builds the memory with the one
-- allocation int a[4] (16 bytes, alignment 4, base 65536; stored as 24 bytes: the last window's
-- 8 more), runs sum-c at x86-64-sysv with p = &a[0], and returns (status , value): 0 and the
-- sum; 1 undefined behaviour; 2 a stop; 3 any other way out.
-- The fuel is a parameter on purpose: at a literal fuel the specializer unrolls the loop that
-- many times (the recursion is on the fuel), so the fuel must be a run-time value.
sum-mem-of : Bytes 24 → AMem
sum-mem-of b =
  AMem { mem-allocs = cons (Alloc { al-id = #0 ; al-size = #16 ; al-align = #4 ; al-live = true
                                  ; al-kind = ak-auto ; al-base = #65536 ; al-zeroed = true
                                  ; al-data = b ; al-init = bytes 24 }) nil
       ; mem-count = #1 ; mem-next = #65553 }
